<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">C. V. Raman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman was an Indian physicist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a>, known for his work on the scattering of light. While working as an accountant in the Indian Finance Service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, he did research at the Indian Association for the Cultivation of Science, and in 1917 became the first Palit Professor of Physics. On 28 February 1928 he and his student K. S. Krishnan discovered that light passing through a transparent material changes its wavelength, the Raman effect, which won him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics in 1930. He became the first Indian director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-institute-of-science">Indian Institute of Science</a> in 1933 and later founded the Raman Research Institute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/c-v-raman.jpg" alt="C. V. Raman" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nobel Foundation · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_CV_Raman.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raman was born on 7 November 1888, the second of eight children of R. Chandrasekhar Iyer and Parvathi Ammal. His father was a school teacher on a modest salary, who in 1892 took a post in physics at a college in Visakhapatnam. There Raman attended St Aloysius' Anglo-Indian High School, passed matriculation at eleven and the First Examination in Arts at thirteen, and took first place in both. He published a first paper on the diffraction of light in 1906, and in February 1907 took first place in the entrance examination for the Indian Finance Service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Posted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, Raman was impressed by the Indian Association for the Cultivation of Science, founded in 1876 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s first research institute. With Ashutosh Mukherjee's support he won permission to do research there in his own time. His article on Newton's rings in polarised light, published in Nature in 1907, was the institute's first research paper. Between 1916 and 1921 he published on bowed string instruments and the wolf tone in violins and cellos.</p><p>During a voyage in September 1921, with a pocket spectroscope and a Nicol prism, he saw that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> looked bluer than the sky's reflection would explain, contradicting Lord Rayleigh's account. He and K. S. Krishnan then sought a scattering with a changed wavelength, and found it on 28 February 1928, a day now marked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as National Science Day. Raman moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bengaluru</a> in 1933 as the first Indian director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-institute-of-science">Indian Institute of Science</a> and founded the Raman Research Institute in 1948.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Grigory Landsberg and Leonid Mandelstam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> independently reported the effect in 1928 and were nominated with Raman in 1930. The Nobel Committee held that they had seen it only in crystals, while Raman and Krishnan had shown it in solids, liquids and gases. Raman also disagreed for decades with Max Born, who worked at the Institute in 1933 and 1934, on lattice dynamics, and most physicists favoured Born's view. He resigned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>, to which he was elected in 1924, in 1968.</p><p>He had a cardiac arrest in his laboratory at the end of October 1970 and died on 21 November 1970, aged 82. Two days earlier he urged a former student not to let the Academy's journals die. His honours included the Hughes Medal in 1930, the Bharat Ratna in 1954 and the Lenin Peace Prize in 1957.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “C. V. Raman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
